\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
safeincludegraphics[width=0.95
\backslash
textwidth,keepaspectratio]{
method_explainer_generations/wpm}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Schematic of the Wave Propagation Method (WPM) as presented by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
after ""
before ""
key "Schmidt2016"
literal "false"

\end_inset

. The input wave is propagated using a bank of homogeneous angular-spectrum kernels, each evaluated at a different representative refractive index 
\begin_inset Formula $n_m$
\end_inset

. At each output pixel, the propagated field corresponding to the local refractive index is selected via mask and recombined. Our improvement to this algorithm (not described in this figure for simplicity) includes a soft binning step which makes this method suitable for a continuous refractive index distribution - see Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:wpm_binning_diagnostics"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 below.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:wpm_explainer"

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Wave Propagation Method (WPM) gathers the split-step approximations in F-MS AS-MS in a one-step propagation, by including the phase shift of the potential in the propagation kernel itself. Indeed, as it is explained below, starting from the local refractive index of the medium, new local wave vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf k(x,y)$
\end_inset

 are defined and use in the propagation step.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Returning to the full second-order Helmholtz equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:helmholtz_form"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

, it can be rearranged into an operator equation with a forward and backward branch 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
after ""
before ""
key "Schmidt2016"
literal "false"

\end_inset

. Ignoring the backward branch, the forward branch may be written as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\label{eq:one_way_square_root_intro}
\partial_z\psi
=
2\pi i\left[
\sqrt{k_0^2n^2(x,y,z)+\frac{1}{4\pi^2}\nabla_\perp^2}-k_0
\right]\psi .
\end{equation}
\end_inset

The central concern is that the square-root operator inside the wave equation mixes the potential and the transverse derivative, and cannot be evaluated as a single Fourier-space propagator. Padé BPM and FC-MS methods solve for this operator using rational or series approximations to the square-root term, which are expensive and cannot usually directly use the FFT.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Looking again at the AS-MS propagation kernel of equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:asm_step"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left[\sqrt{k_0^2-k_\perp^2}-k_0\right]
\]
\end_inset

what is primarily missing is a refractive-index term. We can reintroduce this term locally by replacing 
\begin_inset Formula $k_0$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $k_0n(x,y)$
\end_inset

, giving
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\left[\sqrt{k_0^2n^2(x,y)-k_\perp^2}-k_0\right].
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a single refractive-index value, this kernel can be evaluated straightforwardly with the angular spectrum method. The WPM simply extends the angular spectrum method by evaluating a bank of homogeneous angular-spectrum propagators for each refractive index in the slice, and then retaining the propagated field corresponding to the local refractive index at each output pixel.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This operation is obviously simplistic, because output pixels can receive a propagated field that travelled from some pixels with the incorrect refractive index. However, the approximation lives in the fact that those different refractive indices are further away from the local refractive-index value of that pixel, and therefore contribute less to the final result. In this manner, the propagation step accounts for the local wavelength inside the specimen without explicitly evaluating the full square-root operator. Schmidt et al.
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
after ""
before ""
key "Schmidt2016"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 showed that the leading error from neglecting coupling between neighbouring refractive-index regions affects amplitude rather than phase; consequently, the WPM phase propagation is correct up to second order.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:wpm_explainer"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 shows the same local-homogeneous idea in the form used by our electron-microscopy adaptation below. In the classical WPM as implemented by Schmidt, a mask is created to separate out each unique refractive index in a slice, the angular spectrum kernel is calculated for each refractive index, and then the mask is used to choose the correct angular spectrum solution from the bank of all computed propagations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Brenner's original formulation
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
after ""
before ""
key "Brenner1993"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, evaluating a different propagation kernel at every output pixel is prohibitively expensive. For a transverse grid with 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 pixels, the direct mixed real/Fourier-space calculation scales as 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal O(P^2)$
\end_inset

. Schmidt et al.
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
after ""
before ""
key "Schmidt2016"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 made the method practical for many optical simulations by grouping pixels with the same refractive index. Only one homogeneous angular-spectrum propagation is then required for each distinct index value. If a slice contains 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 such values, the cost is reduced to approximately 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathcal O(MP\log P),
\]
\end_inset

instead of a direct all-to-all evaluation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This saving is large in light-optical simulations where a slice may contain only a small number of unique refractive indices. It is less immediate in electron microscopy, where the electrostatic potential varies continuously and almost every pixel may have a different refractive index. Exact grouping therefore gives little benefit unless the refractive-index field is deliberately discretised. A second issue is that hard grouping introduces sharp masks, which are inconvenient for gradient-based optimisation because the assignment of pixels to refractive-index groups is not smoothly differentiable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Wave Propagation Multislice
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:wp_ms"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We refer to our adaptation of the WPM suitable for electron microscopy as wave propagation multislice (WP-MS), to distinguish it from the classical WPM used in scalar light optics. To make the computation of WP-MS tractable in the context of electron microscopy, and to avoid the naive 
\begin_inset Formula $O(N^{2D})$
\end_inset

 calculation for a 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

-dimensional transverse grid, we adapt partially the WPM algorithm presented in
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
after ""
before ""
key "BarreBrunel2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

. Their method makes use of a soft-partition mask between a slice with two unique refractive index values to maintain differentiability of the WPM algorithm. However, they note that they can only use this mask because they have chosen an object with two refractive index values, and an extension to a more general case would be more challenging.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To adapt this method to a slice with more refractive index values, we first discretise the refractive-index range 
\begin_inset Formula $[n_{\mathrm{min}}, n_{\mathrm{max}}]$
\end_inset

 of the current slice into 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\mathrm{bins}}$
\end_inset

 discrete reference values, denoted as 
\begin_inset Formula $\{n_{\mathrm{ref}}^{(k)}\}_{k=1}^{N_{\mathrm{bins}}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In a typical multislice potential, many of the refractive-index values will be very close to 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 in the vacuum region, while the refractive-index values inside an atom will concentrate in a narrow range slightly above 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

. Since there are likely to be many more refractive-index values near the vacuum region, and the high refractive index values of the atoms are likely to be more sparsely distributed (as the atoms take up comparatively less space in a slice), we employ a quadratic distribution for the reference indices rather than using linearly spaced bins, in order to allocate more bins near 
\begin_inset Formula $n=1$
\end_inset

. The reference indices are distributed according to a quadratic function determined by the parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

, with 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=2.0$
\end_inset

 for quadratic spacing:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\label{eq:wpm_bin_spacing}
n_{\mathrm{ref}}^{(k)}
=
n_{\mathrm{min}}
+
(n_{\mathrm{max}} - n_{\mathrm{min}})
\left( \frac{k-1}{N_{\mathrm{bins}}-1} \right)^\gamma,
\quad
k=1,\ldots,N_{\mathrm{bins}},
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which allows for denser binning near the vacuum refractive index when 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma > 1$
\end_inset

 See 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:wpm_binning_diagnostics"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 for a visual diagram showing graphically the binning step of a slice.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Next, for a propagation step 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta z$
\end_inset

, we compute a bank of propagated fields 
\begin_inset Formula $u_k(x,y)$
\end_inset

. Each field 
\begin_inset Formula $u_k$
\end_inset

 corresponds to the diffraction of the input field 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{\mathrm{in}}(x,y)$
\end_inset

 assuming a homogeneous medium with refractive index 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{\mathrm{ref}}^{(k)}$
\end_inset

. This is performed efficiently using batch FFT operations with the angular spectrum kernel:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\label{eq:wpm_binned_fields}
u_k(x,y)
=
\mathcal{F}^{-1}
\left[
\mathcal{F}\{u_{\mathrm{in}}\}
\exp\left(
2\pi i \Delta z
\left[\sqrt{(k_0 n_{\mathrm{ref}}^{(k)})^2-k_x^2-k_y^2}-k_0\right]
\right)
\right].
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To evaluate the field at a specific spatial coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

 where the local refractive index is 
\begin_inset Formula $n(x,y)$
\end_inset

, we identify the two bounding reference bins such that 
\begin_inset Formula $n_L \le n(x,y) < n_R$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $n_L,n_R \in \{n_{\mathrm{ref}}^{(k)}\}$
\end_inset

. We adopt a differentiable interpolation scheme by defining a local interpolation weight
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\label{eq:wpm_raw_weight}
w_{\mathrm{raw}} = \frac{n(x,y) - n_L}{n_R - n_L}, \quad w_{\mathrm{raw}} \in [0, 1]
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To ensure smooth gradients and minimise discretisation artifacts, we map this linear weight through a smoothstep activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $S(w)$
\end_inset

, akin to the spatial partitioning technique demonstrated by
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
after ""
before ""
key "BarreBrunel2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 via a cubic Hermite interpolation function:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\label{eq:wpm_smoothstep}
w = S(w_{\mathrm{raw}}) = 3w_{\mathrm{raw}}^2 - 2w_{\mathrm{raw}}^3
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While linear interpolation would satisfy the requirement for differentiability, it could result in discontinuities in the gradient at the bin boundaries in a gradient-based optimisation scheme.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The final field at the next step 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x,y,z+\Delta z)$
\end_inset

 is computed as the weighted superposition of the fields propagated at the bounding indices:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\label{eq:wpm_two_bin_interpolation}
u(x,y) = (1 - w) \cdot u_L(x,y) + w \cdot u_R(x,y)
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equivalently, the WP-MS update can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\label{eq:wpm_bank}
\psi(x,y,z+\Delta z)
\approx
\sum_{m=1}^{N_{\mathrm{bins}}} w_m(x,y)
\mathcal F^{-1}
\left[
\exp\left(
2\pi i\left[
\sqrt{k_0^2\left(n_{\mathrm{ref}}^{(m)}\right)^2-k_\perp^2}
-k_0
\right]\Delta z
\right)
\mathcal F\{\psi(x,y,z)\}
\right].
\end{equation}
\end_inset

This gives a differentiable approximation to the one-way medium propagator in equation
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:one_way_square_root_intro"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

. When the slice is homogeneous, only one refractive-index bin is needed and WP-MS reduces to the angular spectrum method. For an inhomogeneous specimen, the method is more expensive than AS-MS because many FFT-based propagations are required per slice, but it accounts for the local wavelength inside the specimen rather than propagating every slice with the vacuum wavelength.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This formulation ensures that the propagation model is fully differentiable with respect to the refractive-index map 
\begin_inset Formula $n(x,y)$
\end_inset

, enabling inverse reconstruction of potentials via automatic differentiation. It also means that only a limited number of FFTs, equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\mathrm{bins}}$
\end_inset

, are required per propagation step, significantly reducing the computational cost compared to a naive WPM implementation. The accuracy of WP-MS depends on the number of bins 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\mathrm{bins}}$
\end_inset

 chosen, and will change on a case-by-case basis depending on the refractive-index distribution of the sample. Figure
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:wpm_binning_diagnostics"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 shows a representative diagnostic of the binning procedure. A practical way to mitigate this potential source of error is to perform a test simulation and double the number of bins until the required tolerance is achieved in the slice error.
\end_layout

