\begin_layout Standard
Although ptychographic reconstruction for specimens as thick as this is not yet feasible, our results show that the choice of propagator can change the diffraction-plane amplitudes. Because ptychographic reconstructions are sensitive to systematic amplitude errors, such errors could be absorbed as artifacts or bias the recovered potential. Therefore the choice of propagator may become more consequential as ptychography is pushed toward thicker specimens and higher scattering angles. Given that there is almost no cost associated with the AS-MS method, we at least recommend that it become the default propagator in almost all multislice use cases going forward to minimise another possible source of error in the forward model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The practical advantage of AS-MS is only immediately useful if it does not require a much finer grid than conventional F-MS. The additional sampling concern comes from the angular-spectrum propagation phase, which is steeper than the Fresnel phase at large transverse wavevector. For one propagation step,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\phi_{\mathrm{F}}(k_\perp) = -\pi\frac{\Delta z}{k_0}k_\perp^2,
\qquad
\phi_{\mathrm{ASM}}(k_\perp) =
2\pi\Delta z\left[\sqrt{k_0^2-k_\perp^2}-k_0\right].
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Comparing the phase gradients gives the relative axial sampling penalty
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{\left|d\phi_{\mathrm{ASM}}/dk_\perp\right|}
{\left|d\phi_{\mathrm{F}}/dk_\perp\right|}
=
\frac{k_0}{\sqrt{k_0^2-k_\perp^2}}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This factor is only large when 
\begin_inset Formula $k_\perp$
\end_inset

 approaches 
\begin_inset Formula $k_0$
\end_inset

, where the propagating spectrum approaches the evanescent boundary. It can be written directly in terms of scattering angle because 
\begin_inset Formula $k_\perp/k_0=\sin\theta$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{k_0}{\sqrt{k_0^2-k_\perp^2}}
= \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-\sin^2\theta}}
= \frac{1}{\cos\theta}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under the TEM conditions used here this remains close to unity. For a 200
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

mrad probe, the largest correction to the Fresnel slice-thickness condition is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{1}{\cos(200~\mathrm{mrad})}
\approx 1.02,
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
so the axial sampling bound is tightened by only about 2%. The transverse grid sets a separate limit as it determines the largest scattering angle that can be represented without aliasing. A real-space pixel size 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x$
\end_inset

 gives a maximum transverse spatial frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{\perp,\mathrm{Nyq}}=1/(2\Delta x)$
\end_inset

. Since 
\begin_inset Formula $k_\perp=k_0\sin\theta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_0=1/\lambda$
\end_inset

, the corresponding Nyquist angle is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\theta_{\mathrm{Nyq}}
= \arcsin\!\left(\frac{\lambda}{2\Delta x}\right)
\approx 320~\mathrm{mrad}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here we used 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda \approx 0.025\,\text{\AA}$
\end_inset

 at 200
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

keV and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x = 0.04\,\text{\AA}$
\end_inset

. This angle is still far from the evanescent boundary at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=90^\circ$
\end_inset

, where the square root in the ASM kernel becomes singular. (Jean Luc needs to add a section here about sampling)
\end_layout

