\begin_layout Standard
The results point to the Fresnel free-space propagator as the main limitation of F-MS in the high-angle and thick-specimen regimes tested here. In the Au
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

[100] ODE benchmark, the discrepancy appears first in the 135
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

mrad beam and only later in the central beam, after the incorrectly propagated diffracted components have had enough thickness to interfere back into it. In the Si
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

[111] CBED calculation, the F-MS peak displacements follow the parabolic-versus-exact dispersion error, while AS-MS remains close to WP-MS over the measured angular range. In the thick Au
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

[100] CBED calculation, the F-MS amplitude error becomes roughly an order of magnitude larger than the AS-MS error beyond about 10
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

nm. These observations indicate that the principal F-MS error is the accumulated longitudinal phase error from the parabolic Fresnel propagator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
AS-MS removes most of the error in the F-MS method by changing only the propagator in the propagation step. The Fresnel kernel is replaced by the exact one-way angular-spectrum kernel, while the transmission step and FFT structure are unchanged. WP-MS makes a further correction by allowing the propagation kernel to depend on the local electrostatic refractive index, so the wavelength can change with the projected potential inside each slice. This makes WP-MS the more complete one-way split-step model, and it is closest to the ODE reference in the Au
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

[100] benchmark. Under the high-energy conditions considered here, however, this extra local-wavelength correction is smaller than the correction obtained by removing the Fresnel approximation. For these specimens and energies, the main source of error is the paraxial free-space dispersion relation rather than the absence of a potential-dependent wavelength in the propagation step.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The silicon calculation shows that the same propagator error is not limited to the most severe high-
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 case. Silicon scatters more weakly than gold, so the CBED amplitude difference is smaller than in the thick Au
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

[100] calculation. However, the F-MS peak shifts still follow the angular dispersion error, while AS-MS remains close to WP-MS across the measured range. A conventional image simulation can hide part of this error if the transfer function or objective aperture suppresses the affected Fourier components, whereas CBED and ptychographic forward models will use the diffraction-plane intensities directly and the error between methods will be more prominent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In particular in a ptychographic reconstruction, the forward model predicts detector intensities for each probe position. If the propagator gives the wrong high-angle amplitudes, peak positions, or accumulated phase through a thick specimen, the optimization can compensate through the recovered potential. The thick Au
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

[100] CBED calculation shows that this modeling error is much larger for F-MS than for AS-MS after approximately 10
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

nm. For forward models that already use F-MS, most of the wide-angle correction can be recovered by replacing 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(-i\pi k_\perp^2 \Delta z/k_0)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(2\pi i\Delta z[\sqrt{k_0^2-k_\perp^2}-k_0])$
\end_inset

, while keeping the same FFT structure, transmission step, and per-slice computational complexity. The sampling estimate above shows that the corresponding sampling penalty is only a few percent under the TEM conditions considered here.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The comparison here is limited to high-energy, forward-scattering calculations where the free-space dispersion error is larger than the local wavelength correction. WP-MS should be tested separately at lower accelerating voltages, for thicker high-
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 specimens, and for slice thicknesses where the potential-dependent wavelength change becomes a comparable source of phase error. Its main advantage in those tests is that it provides a differentiable one-way method with a propagation kernel that depends on the local electrostatic refractive index, that can make use of the computational efficiency of the Fourier transform
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Conclusion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We adapted the optimised Wave Propagation Method developed in light optics to electron microscopy, giving a wave propagation multislice (WP-MS) method that tracks high-angle propagation and local wavelength changes within a differentiable split-step framework, that can make efficient use of the Fourier transform. We then compared F-MS, AS-MS, and WP-MS in three complementary settings: an Au
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

[100] ODE benchmark, a lower-
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 Si
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

[111] CBED calculation, and a thick Au
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

[100] CBED calculation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Across these tests, replacing the Fresnel kernel with the exact angular-spectrum kernel accounts for most of the improvement over conventional multislice. WP-MS is consistently the most complete one-way model and is closest to the ODE reference in the Au
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

[100] benchmark, but its additional accuracy over AS-MS is small under the high-energy conditions tested here. The main practical recommendation is that for high-angle elastic forward simulations where F-MS is still being used, AS-MS should be the default standard, because it removes the dominant paraxial dispersion error without changing the basic FFT-based multislice workflow.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Future work could extend the WP-MS toward a bidirectional formulation that can include backscattering, and then benchmark it against FC-MS in lower-energy simulations, and measure how F-MS, AS-MS, and WP-MS affect ptychographic reconstructions across specimen thickness, accelerating voltage, and atomic number.
\end_layout

